"Here's the spec I'd commit to before building anything:\n\n**Purpose**\nA project status report gives stakeholders a fast, honest snapshot of where the work stands: progress vs. plan, milestone status, risks/issues, and decisions needed. Its job is decision support — a reader should grasp overall health in under two minutes and know exactly what (if anything) needs their action. It's a communication instrument, not documentation.\n\n**Audience**\n- **Primary:** project sponsor / management — time-poor, mixed technical fluency, scanning for RAG status, risks, budget/schedule drift, and open asks.\n- **Secondary:** delivery team and adjacent stakeholders (PMO, client contacts) who want the detail behind the summary — timeline, scope changes, blockers.\n\nDesign implication: layered structure. Executive summary and status tiles up top; detail and risk register below. Minimal jargon, everything defined on first use.\n\n**Native file format**\nSingle self-contained **HTML file**. Rationale:\n- Fixed, professional styling that renders identically everywhere\n- Opens on any OS with zero dependencies — no Office license, no viewer app\n- Browser \"Print to PDF\" gives you a formal distribution artifact for free\n- Small enough to email as an attachment\n\nI can also produce **PDF or DOCX** on request if your workflow demands an editable or locked format.\n\n**Consumer environment assumptions**\n- Modern evergreen browser (Chrome/Edge/Firefox/Safari); desktop-first, readable on tablet\n- Fully offline: inline CSS, system font stack, no CDN, no network calls\n- No JavaScript required for core content (JS only for progressive niceties, if any)\n- A4/Letter print-safe with a print stylesheet and clean page breaks\n- No server hosting assumed — the file must survive being forwarded as an attachment\n\nGive me the project name, reporting period, and your raw status data (a rough brain-dump is fine), and I'll build the report and populate it."